#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101725} :activity/id #uuid "6bc26614-e494-5e0e-b8e8-0405e49dbaa9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578 :topic/id #uuid "aae56e33-e57d-5843-b31c-fcba98a1ca50" :topic/math-academy-id 1163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102033 :tutorial/content "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $y=y(t)$ is called a **logistic growth equation.** \n\nNote the following:\n\n- $a$ is a positive constant called the **carrying capacity**.\n\n- $k$ is a positive constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below:\n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nWe will learn more about the structure of the solution curves in future lessons. But for now, it's helpful to realize that for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 < y(t) < a.\n$$\n\n \n\nAn example of a logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = 10y(50-y).\n$$\n\nHere, $k = 10$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5791475-4f91-5859-9d5b-1f20283522ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 982 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a86bd27c-35f7-56a0-9dd4-1415fb4b1ae3" :step/math-academy-id 1925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100777 :question/id #uuid "a16c2c4f-38c3-5e8c-8962-3ad6c956748a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531568}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"} :step/id #uuid "fa88498c-b724-5dfa-b5a3-3f4d0dbea254" :step/math-academy-id 27104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100779 :question/id #uuid "29ee4686-7e38-5000-9d89-58e572f84cd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7404"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523982}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "257b95d0-a448-51aa-936a-9acc386bda17" :step/math-academy-id 27468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101996 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to create a model of the deer population in a forest. Let's denote the number of deer in the forest at time $t$ as $P(t).$\n\nImagine that we have two factors that influence the size of the deer population:\n\n- Unconstrained, the rate at which the deer population increases is proportional to the size of the deer population (i.e., exponential growth).\n\n- However, as the deer population increases, food resources become more scarce. The forest cannot sustain an arbitrarily large number of deer. Therefore, we must specify the maximum number of deer that the forest can sustain. This maximum number is the forest's carrying capacity, denoted $a,$ as before.\n\nTherefore, we say that the rate of change of the deer population $P(t)$ is **jointly proportional** to *both* the deer population $P$ itself *and* the remaining room for growth, which is $a-P.$ This gives us the logistic growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = kP(a-P).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "136097ca-5c5c-587e-918d-decbcb156cbf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5720 :tutorial/title "Modeling Situations Using a Logistic Growth Equation"} :step/id #uuid "80e2d4b5-6d7a-5649-8978-26fea11f0ae7" :step/math-academy-id 35117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101785}}] :activity/title "Logistic Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101030}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524072} :activity/id #uuid "85f9efd2-54dd-542f-9fb0-3457207d9896" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578 :topic/id #uuid "aae56e33-e57d-5843-b31c-fcba98a1ca50" :topic/math-academy-id 1163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a356ff5-da1f-5dea-9c22-ac42a978f1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.2P(300-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250914} {:answer/id #uuid "3882ae11-e665-5f98-b13e-856ea3ff067a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251023} {:answer/id #uuid "65ba5d91-2960-5f9c-b94f-a9c1cfb957b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251388} {:answer/id #uuid "81afc71e-2e81-5e1d-9747-fb7bccbefe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251647} {:answer/id #uuid "f220ea73-cdb5-5817-8387-4873699ce350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251023} :answer-field/id #uuid "225256aa-a878-5dbd-ac6b-c6508ad4090e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6e07b3cd-6441-55ba-89c7-eb3d668f886b" :question/math-academy-id "q-119289" :question/problem "A pest infestation is spreading through a land with $300$ trees. The rate of growth in the number of trees $P(t)$ infected with the pest is jointly proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ measured in months,  and the number of uninfected trees. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of infected trees is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected trees, the number of infected trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the number of trees that are infected is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4245bf2c-f044-5000-a8dc-9e8cf792d0e0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bfabfe5-1bde-5d94-bc4f-bdd3166c3f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=20-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523379} {:answer/id #uuid "46cc8677-e9f5-50f1-957a-d6c09372ed81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523401} {:answer/id #uuid "55c32120-58ea-521e-945f-a4d006827dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523430} {:answer/id #uuid "674fb894-2a3f-511b-92a2-711f65663bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523466} {:answer/id #uuid "80937afc-7ea6-5bca-890e-a680c897e7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523430} :answer-field/id #uuid "c929a608-d503-51e7-8e16-80118c54e14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "893452ea-005e-5fa0-9579-b213ff801fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83846" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=20.$"} :step/id #uuid "576ab541-b8f7-5797-923d-f3afc45640a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d85e959-a5d2-57fb-865e-ce7038ba9c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(500-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523283} {:answer/id #uuid "3d57d462-ef3d-519d-9fd5-bf23c60439b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523383} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3d47cb-be7b-539e-8964-2c16730aedcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=500-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523509} {:answer/id #uuid "cf651d75-4fcd-5021-9ec0-003f1d0c2149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523689} {:answer/id #uuid "e6223966-2351-5f3e-86f0-c546dcff0000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523689} :answer-field/id #uuid "09aa265b-d358-5ee1-8520-4f4d7c406505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d36aaa4-be3b-5e83-9941-c6527d829fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83754" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$"} :step/id #uuid "845e97b0-decc-50f2-997f-93c6453f8cf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c7ca84a-cf9f-50f8-b341-b5cad100965c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.6P(700\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523411} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee52ea6-c780-5145-af3b-00bf2afb2de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=700\\,000P(0.6-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523481} {:answer/id #uuid "b251d084-9823-5f88-b5bc-44200f475b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=700\\,000P(0.6+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523630} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb1b6f5-2157-5ffa-857f-384cd5652759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523684} {:answer/id #uuid "cedd52a2-345c-58b2-928e-6c0218a680ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523684} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33bf6b7-b2a2-5c1d-93df-e2640b2be937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f74431a-7f46-5af6-affc-22cbe39ff9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-83865" :question/problem "An influenza epidemic is spreading through a community of $700\\,000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with the flu is jointly proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ measured in days,  and the number of uninfected people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.6,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of infected people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected people, the number of infected people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.6,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=700\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have the flu is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000-P).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "876b041e-48ee-59ed-9570-27aae1e87501" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bba37af-8df4-5b8c-a930-80d2186439c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251435} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe50a92-b71b-5769-a939-63ed274c1a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252210} {:answer/id #uuid "dead85fa-fe2f-53e8-b67b-e9c7315851fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252516} {:answer/id #uuid "fd955712-55d7-5520-b9cd-82c788b4c2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=50-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252778} {:answer/id #uuid "fe08bd02-4725-5feb-946c-394fc94ff25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4(50-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251435} :answer-field/id #uuid "569731a1-44d9-5454-8f2b-b1754aaefd2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19423d48-c141-516d-a17c-c34914d7472c" :question/math-academy-id "q-119264" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$"} :step/id #uuid "c8f38971-defd-50cd-9dce-38d71497ce0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524056}}] :activity/title "Logistic Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523243}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069905} :activity/id #uuid "347053fb-b31c-5623-b4db-4c770ada4d18" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049069 :topic/id #uuid "d7927711-e084-5b0c-97d6-05acd9686522" :topic/math-academy-id 416} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070031 :tutorial/content "We know that we can use the method of integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Now, suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2e^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Can we use integration by parts in this case?\n\nLet's  try. We define $u$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for integration by parts leads to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe still don't have an answer, but the problem is reduced to the calculation of a simpler integral  $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ To compute this integral, we just need to apply the integration by parts formula again. For this second integral, we take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{8}\\,x{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}-\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}(8{x}^{2}-4x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{C_1}{2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "35a2eb7b-569b-5939-bcc0-2261596617e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 901 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "944ebf60-fe41-58ac-91a8-63c93b565add" :step/math-academy-id 655420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068636 :question/id #uuid "8ec71af3-4534-5ef1-8745-3220102ba838" :question/math-academy-id "e-7053"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426573}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"} :step/id #uuid "f13b1e1c-2034-5d26-b6a0-2b5227d65b96" :step/math-academy-id 655421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068571 :question/id #uuid "c1dcb0a7-441f-5628-ba23-71d43d9ea96c" :question/math-academy-id "e-495"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426575}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"} :step/id #uuid "c22a3f17-9151-565b-8158-62703c1da221" :step/math-academy-id 655424 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068572 :question/id #uuid "b81ff533-2067-5d1a-b265-df1ad91fa8da" :question/math-academy-id "e-496"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377594}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"} :step/id #uuid "fbb28f81-ea51-5172-9911-15b8c5ea0ef5" :step/math-academy-id 655427}] :activity/title "Applying the Integration By Parts Twice" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068951}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176717} :activity/id #uuid "2a069471-7d01-5a03-97db-297443dadb35" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049491 :topic/id #uuid "f90d8601-e439-5cfc-9a0b-afc8462d4f0a" :topic/math-academy-id 3632} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175977 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175827 :question/id #uuid "6a090caa-4c73-5f90-9cb1-dc7dcdfe9e37" :question/math-academy-id "e-8906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434227}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "1ab1f1c7-2015-5a88-9ddc-d4b547073338" :step/math-academy-id 33266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176988 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis. We can also obtain surfaces of revolution by rotating a curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis.\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=f(y)$ that lies between the horizontal lines $y=y_1$ and $y=y_2.$ By rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we get a surface of revolution, as shown below on the right. How can we calculate its surface area?\n\n![](images/e6/e61f97b06c3228bb2671429d6562e97d6cdefefb7ac1acca31867eeb440a75ff.png)\n\nFirst, we divide the interval $[y_1,y_2]$ into small subintervals, each of length $\\Delta{y}.$ Let's consider a part of the curve that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of the corresponding curved arc be $\\Delta{s}.$ When this part of the curve is rotated about the $y$-axis, it forms a closed strip. \n\n![](images/25/253837a6c151a74318b2e172e6db068a50d23c9cb93124588ae20a9379d9aa63.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{y}$ is very small, we can assume that both left and right radii of the strip are approximately equal to $x=f(y).$ Then, this strip can be viewed as the lateral surface of a right cylinder with height $h=\\Delta{s}$ and radius $r=x=f(y).$ \n\n![](images/9f/9f10812beff0283e6029145b854a2faaa9b7a566751bd17514bc6817ef664dfc.png)\n\nWe can approximate the lateral surface area of such a cylinder as\n\n$$\nS_{\\text{strip}} \\approx S_{\\text{cylinder}} = 2\\pi r \\cdot h = 2\\pi x \\cdot \\Delta{s}.\n$$\n\nThe sum over all subintervals will approximate the total area $S$ that we want. The approximation becomes more and more accurate as $\\Delta{s} \\to 0$. Therefore, to get the area of the surface, we need to integrate:\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nRecall that for a curve $x = f(y),$ we can express the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ as\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find the total surface area using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1dd2211-511b-5b65-9250-ee6b84f0d566" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5319 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "76d50374-b9a8-5d13-90fe-031de2946fbe" :step/math-academy-id 33267 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175978 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175828 :question/id #uuid "6ee942e8-9fb9-5dfb-8f9c-aea1dd6d53ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050393}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434226}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "b8f345d7-4e41-5e19-b9f6-352cfd48cc6a" :step/math-academy-id 33268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175992 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175843 :question/id #uuid "d0546997-c726-5ec2-ae10-c2373ed8b863" :question/math-academy-id "e-9121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434228}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "29ad360c-8446-5b96-ba6b-4be86cac5c72" :step/math-academy-id 33994 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177009 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given a curve of the form $y=f(x)$ that lies between the vertical lines $x=x_1$ and $x=x_2.$ How do we calculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\nEarlier, we showed that the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nFor a curve $y = f(x),$ the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the surface that is obtained when the $y=f(x)$ curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b0c21ed5-0845-50c6-ba7b-5e4581b700a8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5537 :tutorial/title "Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "e55d411a-f21b-5007-bf69-39adfb956fe6" :step/math-academy-id 34386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176742}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution: Rotation About the Y-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176149}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148235} :activity/id #uuid "8898632a-f568-581b-9193-aeb177e79e44" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049952 :topic/id #uuid "20d8e368-2cd0-5f3b-ab4b-9db7029241aa" :topic/math-academy-id 2512} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148738 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a tank is filling up with water, and the rate at which water flows into the tank is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 600e^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per hour, and $t > 0$ is the time (in hours) since the water flow started. \n\nSuppose we want to know how much water enters the tank between $1$ and $2$ hours. \n\nLet's take note of the following:\n\n- We're interested in the amount of water that enters the tank between $t=1$ and $t=2.$ In other words, we want the **net change in volume** between these times. We're *not* finding the total volume of water in the tank.\n\n- We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$\n\nTo find the net change in volume, we integrate $r(t)$ between the two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}600{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{600}{3}{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={-200{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-200({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-3}) \\\\  & =200({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-6}) \\\\  & =200{e}^{-6}({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60a3bf2a-7142-575f-8381-dfb70827c7c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2744 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62e34955-26a4-56d7-b473-2f2540475511" :step/math-academy-id 13518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147333 :question/id #uuid "a3030491-423d-5633-8208-b373bfee3b06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3892"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050200}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478466}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "98d275b2-d6f2-53c5-b4d7-cae25b562f1a" :step/math-academy-id 13519 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148739 :tutorial/content "If we're interested to find the **total number** or **total amount** of some quantity, we should use the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nLet's go back to the earlier example with the shopping mall (Example 1). Suppose that there are $2\\,000$ people inside the mall at the moment that it opened. How many people in total are inside the mall after $2$ hours?\n\nWe already know that the **rate of change** in the number of people is $R(t).$ Therefore, the **total number of people** in the mall at time $t$, which we'll call $T(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: You'll notice that we changed the variable of integration from $t$ to $x.$ This is to ensure that $T$ comes out as a function of $t,$ and also so we don't get confused. When we have a definite integral, it does not matter which variable we use!\n\nTo work out how many people are inside the mall at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\,000+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{850} \\\\  & =2\\,000+850 \\\\  & =2\\,850\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02950c6c-bf43-5c34-885c-8da2caa06fbf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2745 :tutorial/title "Finding Total Amounts Given Rates of Change"} :step/id #uuid "2755fa08-44ef-5ef5-b8c8-6470c68e446a" :step/math-academy-id 13520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147334 :question/id #uuid "d007e9ad-3fd3-5ae2-864a-ef89ea01785c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3893"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506580}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "abe470e8-0b76-56c6-9c15-a1d10e391c4b" :step/math-academy-id 13521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147335 :question/id #uuid "74125c40-ecdd-5453-9c49-13567fb4621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3894"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048498}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506541}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "5d3cad10-c38a-51fd-b011-83786859f767" :step/math-academy-id 13522}] :activity/title "Integrating Rates of Change" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147695}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506631} :activity/id #uuid "b0847fb5-fa32-5a94-94b5-243ac836859c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049952 :topic/id #uuid "20d8e368-2cd0-5f3b-ab4b-9db7029241aa" :topic/math-academy-id 2512} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ae082c6-947e-5375-a9f4-aa88253e382d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "188" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318032}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318032} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc1a0df-9be5-533e-b00c-ee8fecf41b26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06774482-8185-522a-b0da-abe466cad0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317591} :answer-field/id #uuid "57c75693-c5b3-5428-8192-d75e3b45beab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b0963b7-9111-575b-994d-33c581e812aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-349996" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours."} :step/id #uuid "25fcc6e6-29c0-5bf9-9f04-6426e5b05635" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c10ece-c009-56c5-be8d-90e6cb75497c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330238} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7ffa2f-9e56-5efe-97c7-d12ccab36064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $0$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330645} {:answer/id #uuid "522f2b59-ef0a-5794-8023-90cf98db584b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The minimum is $8$ participants  after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330698} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eb419f-421f-54a3-b0b8-0034e420363a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $0$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331864} {:answer/id #uuid "f18e8e04-35b4-55b7-86fa-527938e47ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332074}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330698} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9450f60-3299-5ac4-9582-ce92545753e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28fcc32f-48c3-5813-8388-8eda5e584ffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-62834" :question/problem "The total number of participants $S(t)$ registered for an online conference after $t$ days is given by the function\n\n$$\nS(t)=40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in days, since the conference was announced. \n\nAt what time is the number of participants at a minimum, and how many participants are registered at this time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number participants is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4x-16=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many participants are registered when $t=4,$ we figure out $S(4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+{[2{x}^{2}-16x]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =40+[(2(4)^{2}-16(4))-(0)] \\\\  & =40-32 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $8<40$, we conclude that the minimum number of participants is $8$, and this occurs after $4$ days."} :step/id #uuid "6c504d06-98a0-51e0-a67b-97bcf359f7c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6f34e98-745c-508e-9aac-c7930cd0847b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506332}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506332} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb50e03-36f7-5d1f-9178-33250a489690" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b1c05acb-14b2-5a23-a0fb-57fef367611e" :question/math-academy-id "q-349973" :question/problem "The rate at which water flows into a tank is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=6t+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{p.m}.$\nWater is also draining from the tank at a steady rate of $8$ liters per hour.\n\nIf there were $200$ liters of water in the tank at $1\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many liters of water will be there at $5\\,\\text{p.m}?$\n\nThere will be \n{{field-1}}  liters of water in the tank at $5\\,\\text{p.m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Water is flowing into the tank at a rate of $6t+10$ liters per hour and is draining at a rate of $8$ liters per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the amount of water (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-8 \\\\  & =6t+10-8 \\\\  & =6t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total amount of water** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the amount of water after $4$ hours ($5\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=4$ into the above and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(4) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(6x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(3{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =200+[(3\\cdot 16+8)-0] \\\\  & =200+56 \\\\  & =256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $256$ liters of water in the tank at $5\\,\\text{p.m}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a55587c2-e7e9-5369-8800-e5f86ed901d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfaac65-5999-5ebb-b5c5-894a0344cbaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505939} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f4130c-9394-541b-a643-051a656f347b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b09585d-43e7-56cb-8602-90e62261bd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506184} :answer-field/id #uuid "265736ea-5f99-5ebe-9b6c-32c27324902a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "54f0409c-f2ef-5cc4-b4e4-ffc38eb896a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-349997" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(10-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4(10-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=5\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=5.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=5,$ we figure out $N(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(5) & =30+4{\\int }_{0}^{5}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =30+4{[10x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =30+4[(10\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =30+4\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =130\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $30<130$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $130$, and this will happen after $5$ hours."} :step/id #uuid "c49eac7d-056b-55ba-a0ba-a1b3b42a5b43"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9635b2b-705b-58e9-94bd-88166205012c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t^{2}-4t+100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506247} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaf115f-1435-52c2-b951-5e9b2f69cb63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7896667b-b339-512f-9dbb-66fd90855442" :question/math-academy-id "q-349984" :question/problem "A city is tracking the number of bikes available in a bike-share station.\nThey observe that bikes are returned to the station at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=5{t}^{3}+2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in bikes per hour, and $t>0$ is the time in hours since tracking began.\nAt the same time, $4$ bikes are rented from the station each hour.\n\nIf there were initially $100$ bikes at the station, find the expression that gives $B(t),$ the number of bikes at the station $t$ hours after tracking began.\n\n$B(t)=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The number of bikes is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ bikes per hour and is decreasing at a rate of $4$ bikes per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of bikes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-4 \\\\  & =5{t}^{3}+2t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $B(t)$ of bikes after $t$ hours, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =B(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(5{x}^{3}+2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =100+{\\int }_{0}^{t}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100+{5\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{t}+{2\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{t}-4x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =100+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}+\\,{t}^{2}-4t+100\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d54dc8bc-ee1a-54b4-8dbf-4d22b96df654" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506613}}] :activity/title "Integrating Rates of Change" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505912}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093309} :activity/id #uuid "59e47f38-9dc9-538d-993c-2e3ee834c641" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050779 :topic/id #uuid "66051139-bfd1-53ce-b0e2-5c7d415815e5" :topic/math-academy-id 999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093361 :tutorial/content "To calculate the arc length of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ between the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the arc length of the polar curve $r = 2,$ where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ The required arc length is shown in blue in the picture below.\n\n![](images/e9/e91cd717469db56426e39e8975aa04710ff2357c7a0d5de9d20f99bc5edcbb1c.png)\n\nWe will use the formula to compute the arc length. Since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 0,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the arc length can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}2\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46d71df2-8000-5586-bb24-748b23a02ce3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1141 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc4e82f8-a2bd-59bb-ab26-acf1b38e6eba" :step/math-academy-id 617629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092373 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092125 :question/id #uuid "8b8e3c66-fc60-57ac-baf7-0661e213d01e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7400"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050277}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531626}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "fb73f248-2c4e-50c5-b636-329ca36abf27" :step/math-academy-id 617630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092374 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092093 :question/id #uuid "57034318-5a6e-53f6-a8d3-ae8c61692947" :question/math-academy-id "e-641"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531644}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "4d753f5d-a62d-581e-9242-d4c1c4d4bef5" :step/math-academy-id 617633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093364 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula to compute the arc length bounded by a polar curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ r^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} \\right)^2} \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's start with the familiar formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nA polar curve is really just a parametric curve with parameter $\\theta$ and parametric equations given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the arc length formula for a parametric function with parameter $\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $x = r \\cos \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since $y=r\\sin \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $L,$ we reach the desired formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1821e80d-8c1b-5d2f-88dd-c207d2c3d99f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1144 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the Main Formula"} :step/id #uuid "a2419f97-10bb-5949-b766-7c403f31ea78" :step/math-academy-id 617636}] :activity/title "The Arc Length of a Polar Curve" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092470}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499588} :activity/id #uuid "caa1b6ea-7850-5c84-a961-1177d87914d7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050779 :topic/id #uuid "66051139-bfd1-53ce-b0e2-5c7d415815e5" :topic/math-academy-id 999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160e2a0c-f074-5874-a5f2-17cc1fc2cae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498777} {:answer/id #uuid "6d20f288-51ab-5e95-ad79-fa5df49f89a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498973} {:answer/id #uuid "a35283a7-4adc-5de1-826d-87a387cd4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} {:answer/id #uuid "c87b6998-b8e9-5934-9893-be14c9c09927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499169} {:answer/id #uuid "d102d768-01d6-510f-bd39-ea2452eac6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafcbac5-e575-5e1d-93e5-5e527facd2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d774fdc1-484c-5e5e-b140-afbc35a348d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51202" :question/problem "![](images/87/87a3563153fe0310767a79a96df0df5f6c0824027776b761df4a792b8b9b235e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2648cacf-aed7-52cf-9142-851e9f4e62e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39bd8942-8ea6-5f03-b9c2-980c08387095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}+{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498858} {:answer/id #uuid "3f95ea7f-6aac-57fb-89fc-e3618b1effd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498876} {:answer/id #uuid "472465ce-5697-565a-b045-a23526f9815c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}({e}^{2\\pi /3}-{e}^{-2\\pi /3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498893} {:answer/id #uuid "a7929dc7-bc71-5a90-8bb2-c5a85e81928b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499099} {:answer/id #uuid "e97e245e-6898-5e7c-8a99-3baaa90c0589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{3\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499099} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f08aa4-9c3b-5881-9036-fcd624473552" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "657b78ff-bfd8-5861-b182-c1815c474783" :question/math-academy-id "q-9534" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=2{e}^{3\\theta }$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{6\\theta }+36{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{40{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4ff166b5-1643-51d7-8f4b-ee7431c1594b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218